@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "SAVEPOINT";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold text-primary">
            SAVEPOINT
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted fs-5">
            Learn how SAVEPOINT is used to create a specific point
            inside a SQL Server transaction and roll back to that point.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS SAVEPOINT?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a SAVEPOINT?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>SAVEPOINT</strong> is a named point created
                inside a transaction.
            </p>

            <p>
                It allows us to roll back only part of a transaction
                instead of rolling back the entire transaction.
            </p>

            <p>
                In SQL Server, the command
                <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong> is used to create
                a savepoint.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY USE SAVEPOINT?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why Do We Use SAVEPOINT?
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To create a specific point inside a transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To roll back only part of a transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To avoid rolling back the complete transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    To provide more control over transaction operations.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                SAVEPOINT Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SAVE TRANSACTION SavePointName;

-- SQL statements

ROLLBACK TRANSACTION SavePointName;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE SAVEPOINT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Creating a SAVEPOINT
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can create a savepoint using the
                <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong> statement.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SAVE TRANSACTION StudentSavePoint;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <strong>StudentSavePoint</strong> is the name
                of the savepoint.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Simple SAVEPOINT Example
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    401,
    'Rahul',
    'Pune',
    22
);

SAVE TRANSACTION StudentSavePoint;

UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Mumbai'
WHERE StudentId = 401;

ROLLBACK TRANSACTION StudentSavePoint;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In this example, the INSERT operation remains part of
                the transaction, while the UPDATE performed after the
                savepoint is rolled back.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                ROLLBACK to a SAVEPOINT
            </h2>

            <p>
                To roll back to a specific savepoint, use
                <strong>ROLLBACK TRANSACTION</strong> followed by
                the savepoint name.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ROLLBACK TRANSACTION StudentSavePoint;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This rolls back the transaction to the specified
                savepoint rather than rolling back the entire transaction.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE SAVEPOINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Multiple SAVEPOINTS
            </h2>

            <p>
                Multiple savepoints can be created during a transaction.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    402,
    'Priya',
    'Pune',
    21
);

SAVE TRANSACTION Point1;

UPDATE Students
SET Age = 22
WHERE StudentId = 402;

SAVE TRANSACTION Point2;

UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Mumbai'
WHERE StudentId = 402;

ROLLBACK TRANSACTION Point2;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, the transaction contains more than one savepoint,
                allowing more precise control over the transaction.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SAVEPOINT VS ROLLBACK
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                SAVEPOINT vs ROLLBACK
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>

                            <th>SAVEPOINT</th>

                            <th>ROLLBACK</th>

                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Creates a point inside a transaction.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Undoes transaction changes.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Allows partial rollback.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Can roll back the entire transaction
                                or to a specified savepoint.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                Created using SAVE TRANSACTION.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Performed using ROLLBACK TRANSACTION.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         REAL WORLD EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Real-World Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider an online order where several operations
                are performed in sequence:
            </p>

            <ol>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Create an order.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Add order items.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Update product stock.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Process additional operations.
                </li>

            </ol>

            <p>
                A savepoint can be created after an important stage.
                If a later operation fails, the transaction can be
                rolled back to that savepoint instead of starting
                everything again.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SQL Server uses <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong>
                    to create a savepoint.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A savepoint must be created inside a transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A transaction can contain multiple savepoints.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ROLLBACK TRANSACTION followed by a savepoint name
                    rolls back to that point.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A savepoint provides more control than rolling back
                    the complete transaction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    COMMIT is used when the final transaction changes
                    should be permanently saved.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p>
            A <strong>SAVEPOINT</strong> is a named point inside a
            transaction. It allows us to roll back a portion of the
            transaction without rolling back everything.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            SQL Server creates savepoints using
            <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong> and rolls back to them
            using <strong>ROLLBACK TRANSACTION</strong>.
        </p>

    </div>


